WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.650
When you type a multi-part command into
the terminal, it feels like a single

2
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:12.870
seamless operation. But the shell actually
splits this line into three distinct,

3
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:17.810
entirely independent programs. These
three processes execute in user mode.

4
00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:22.390
They sit above the strict privilege boundary,
completely isolated from each other.

5
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:28.450
Down below, in kernel mode, the operating
system creates unnamed pipes: circular memory

6
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:34.050
buffers used for passing data. For any
sequence of n commands, the system builds n

7
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:39.470
minus 1 pipes. Right now, these programs in
user mode and the pipes in the kernel are

8
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.050
entirely disconnected. Data cannot flow
until they are wired together in a precise

9
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:49.670
sequence. A command-line pipeline is a
complex construction project: the shell must

10
00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:54.850
orchestrate every connection before a single
byte moves. The shell tackles this one

11
00:00:54.900 --> 00:01:00.310
command at a time. It begins by executing
a pipe system call to create the very first

12
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:05.670
circular buffer in the kernel. Next, the
shell executes fork, duplicating its own

13
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:10.650
process to create a worker for the first
command. This child process must route its

14
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:16.290
output to the new channel. It uses dup2 to
copy the pointer, replacing its standard

15
00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.090
output file descriptor with a connection
directly to the write end of the pipe.

16
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:25.290
Because the child inherits all open file
descriptors from the parent, it now closes

17
00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:30.250
any ends it will not use, severing the extra
connections. With its output locked into

18
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:36.270
the pipeline, the child executes execvp:
the shell process is replaced entirely by

19
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:41.730
the actual target program, ls. Back in the
parent shell, the cleanup continues: the

20
00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:45.930
shell closes the write end of
the pipe it handed to the child,

21
00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:52.050
holding on to only the read end for the next round of
the loop. This combination of dup2 and execvp lets

22
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.850
standard programs write into kernel pipes
blindly. ls simply writes to standard

23
00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:01.810
output, completely unaware that the shell
has redirected that output into a pipe. The

24
00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:06.030
shell repeats this exact sequence for
the rest of the commands: it spawns new

25
00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:10.910
children, redirects their input and output
file descriptors, and transforms them into

26
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:17.890
grep and wc. This leads to the golden rule
of pipelines: every pipe must end up with

27
00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:22.410
exactly one write descriptor and one read
descriptor open among the two processes

28
00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:28.030
that use it. If the parent shell or a child
process fails to close just one unused

29
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.330
file descriptor during the loop, an extra
writer remains attached to the pipe. When an

30
00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:37.190
extra write descriptor stays open, the
kernel assumes more data might arrive;

31
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:42.150
therefore, it never sends an end-of-file
signal. This section of the lab manual

32
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:47.510
outlines an exercise where a single close
is deliberately removed. Without an end-of-

33
00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:52.110
file signal, the final command in the pipeline
waits for data indefinitely, causing a

34
00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:57.810
deadlock. Pipelines fail silently: a single
forgotten close results in an infinite

35
00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:03.470
wait. With all descriptors correctly wired
and the unused ends closed, the shell steps

36
00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.550
back. Data flows continuously across the
privilege boundary, out of one process,

37
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:13.830
through the kernel pipe, and into the next.
The shell's final required action is to

38
00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:18.590
wait. It must actively track and acknowledge
the termination of every child process;

39
00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:23.890
if it doesn't, those finished processes
remain in the system as zombies. To determine

40
00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:28.390
the success or failure of the entire operation,
the shell captures the specific exit

41
00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.690
status of the very last command in the
chain. The vertical bar you type in the

42
00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:36.470
terminal relies entirely on rigorous
file descriptor management and process

43
00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:41.210
composition. Let's review. If
a pipeline has four commands,

44
00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.900
how many pipes does the
shell create in the kernel?

45
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:50.470
Three. n commands always require n minus
1 pipes. Here's the second question: what

46
00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:55.210
happens if a parent process forgets to
close an unused write descriptor? The final

47
00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:59.750
command waits for an end of file that
never comes, causing a deadlock. Now open

48
00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:04.400
animation A5.1 in the
course hub and run lab TD4.
